In a statement reacting to Mr. Khan Sovann's remarks on May 21, 2020, the Board of Priests emphasized that in writing and this statement, the Board of Priests of Cambodia strongly rejects this opinion. Absolutely not acceptable.
In this statement, the Board of Priests considers that Mr. Khan Sovann's opinion embodies the following meanings, contents and reflections:
1: Immorality, filthy thoughts and the most brutal atrocities
2: Insults to Buddhist monks at all levels throughout the country
3: Diverting the masses to follow in the footsteps of bitter history (three years, eight months, one day) is a dark, secular Tamil regime that the world and humanity hate the most.
4: Violation of the Cambodian Penal Code under Article 516 for insulting and disciplining monks and Article 43 of the Cambodian Constitution states that Buddhism is the state religion and the state has an obligation to prevent any person from violating it.
5: It is a universal violation of human rights that enshrines the right to faith and the dignified survival of the clergy and the laity.
6. Thoughts lack thinking, lack of experience, lack of historical knowledge, lack of integrity, lack of justice and lack of dignity in the use of writing and expression.
7: Create pain and bondage to young monks and Buddhists.
The presiding priest added in the statement that although in the present as well as in the past, the sins of a small number of pastors were true, but not all. On the contrary, the Board of Priests of Cambodia has always taken care to take transparent measures to prevent individuals from committing misconduct to continue their priesthood.
The Board of Priests asked Mr. Khan Sovann to take a closer look at the long history of Cambodia that Cambodia has gone through and to see both the good and the bad, the pros and cons for culture, religion and tradition. In particular, you should see the positive and negative points of all generations of monks for the Cambodian society, please do not look only at the bad points, the slight negatives of the monks alone, look at the whole forest, do not look at a single tree.
The statement added that in Cambodian society, the wheels of the Buddha must go hand in hand with the empire, because the Buddha is the source of morality in Cambodian society, the empire is the creator of socio-economic material, so the two wheels must complement each other. Science can not lack morality, and morality can not lack science.
The Board of Priests deeply regrets and condemns Khan Sovann's writings and remarks, calling them a lack of intuition, a lack of common sense, a bias and a pessimistic view of the opposite of social, religious and cultural progress.
It should be noted that for Cambodia, only under the genocidal regime of Pol Pot, all religious beliefs were eliminated and monks were forced to leave the seminary. Since the genocidal regime was overthrown on January 7, 1979, faith and religion have been revived.
The Royal Government of Cambodia, led by the Cambodian People's Party (CPP) and Samdech Techo Hun Sen, as its leader and former pagoda boy, has paid close attention to the Buddhist flag and promoted Buddhism. Samdech Techo has also emphasized that the Buddha and the Empire are an indispensable link.
Mr. Khan Sovann's remarks have led to strong reactions from monks and Buddhists who respect and strongly believe in Buddhism, as defined in the constitution as the nation's religion.
Monks and Buddhists who have reacted consider Khan Sovann's remarks to undermine the value of Buddhism, which is protected by law in Cambodia.
