(Kampong Chhnang): Ms. Ou Amararatna, Director of Saora Organization, trained vocational handicrafts for people with disabilities in Kandal Village, Sangkat Kampong Chhnang, Kampong Chhnang City on May 20, 2020, appealing to local and foreign customers. The country kindly provided financial and material support to Saora.
According to Fresh News, the director said that Saora is only 8 months old and is asking for help to help vocational training for people with disabilities to master the art of weaving by hand to support their livelihoods. Improved in society, avoiding hiding in the house or begging for life.
Ms. Ou Amararath confirmed that Saora was established and registered by the Ministry of Interior on September 9, 2019 after completing a one-year scholarship to learn hand-weaving techniques and learn about work in A leader with a disability in Japan.
In addition, a total of 11 people with disabilities come to receive training today and come to study regularly between 3 to 5 people because the need to bring a family can not travel on their own. Lack of means to transport people with disabilities. There is a lack of learning space in the organization, there is no space for people with disabilities, there are 3 contract staff in the organization and 8 youth volunteers.
Regarding the handicraft products of the disabled people, Ms. Oum Amararatna appealed: "Please call on customers, philanthropists, please donate material or help to buy krama products for the disabled people in the organization. "Disabilities have jobs and better lives." Large-scale Biogas Information and Technology Center will contribute to the development of biogas power on farms
(Phnom Penh): Large-Scale Biogas Information and Technology Center (BTIC) is a large-scale biogas information and technology center as well as providing technical services, including training to stakeholders such as pig farms, project developers, investors, developers. Policymakers, financial institutions and researchers, as well as local engineering companies.
The center will contribute to the development of biogas as a source of clean energy on farms to support rural electricity generation as well as reduce greenhouse gas emissions. Currently, this power source is not yet widely used. The BTIC Center was jointly established by the United Nations Industrial Development Organization (UNIDO) and the Royal University of Agriculture.
The center is supported by the project "Reducing greenhouse gas emissions through the promotion of large-scale biogas plants", which is being implemented jointly by the Ministry of Environment, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and UNIDO with financial support from the Global Environment Facility. Mr. (GEF).
The center was officially opened on December 8, 2016 and is located in the Faculty of Agricultural Engineering of the Royal University of Agriculture (RUA) of Cambodia.
The Large-Scale Biogas Information and Technology Center aims to: * Build national technical capacity, * Disseminate knowledge on large-scale biogas technology * Establish networks with biogas centers and stakeholders in the region.
The center has the vision of becoming a leading hub for sustainable large-scale biogas development in Cambodia. Mission: Provide knowledge and information on new technologies in the field of biogas and related fields, including policy, capacity building and training based on customer needs, consulting services, research and development.
The Large-Scale Biogas Information and Technology Center strives to reduce greenhouse gas emissions, improve agricultural waste management to protect human health and the environment. The Center will work closely with national and international partners to promote the wider use of the best technology available in Cambodia.
