Holidays in Cambodia on May 20 are very important. If May 20 is not a national holiday, today is a day of individual remembrance and commitment. May 20 represents an opportunity to remember that a past or a family, a community or the history of a country can be an opportunity for the next generation to learn and teach.
In 1984, May 20 was designated as "Day of the Rage." May 20 is designated in part as a symbol of opposition to the United Nations. Between 1982 and 1983, more than 1 million Cambodians signed and signed a petition to the United Nations through the Government of the People's Republic of Cambodia.
May 20 is an important day, not only a day off from work, but also an opportunity for exploration and dedication. Teachers, students, government officials, monks, clergy and the general public can gather to pay their last respects to those who lost their lives during the Khmer Rouge regime at monuments near their homes.
Not only that, the "National Day of Remembrance" on May 20 provided the collective and emotional reparations of the Khmer Rouge tribunal. In 2018, the Khmer Rouge Tribunal recognized the date set by the Royal Government for May 20 as a national holiday as compensation for Civil Parties in Case 002/01.
However, May 20, 2020 and the years to come are no longer public holidays in Cambodia. Despite these changes, I hope we can still remember May 20.
On May 20, 2020, Youk Chhang, Director of the Documentation Center of Cambodia, US Ambassador Patrick Murphy, and staff of the US Embassy in Phnom Penh, celebrated the birth of Kosal at Wat Snguon Pich to commemorate the victims. Lost his life. Wat Snguon Pich is located in Snguon Pich village, about 3 km from the Khmer Rouge tribunal.
A small group of monks, abbots and grandparents joined in the traditional Khmer blessing in front of the monument in Wat Snguon Pich to dedicate to those who lost their lives in this pagoda. A small group of seniors share their experiences of the Khmer Rouge regime in front of a memorial.
