(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and the International Rice Research Institute (IRRI) this morning, May 20, 2020, signed a four-year plan document on “Joint Cooperation in Partnership for Cooperation. "Scientific and technical operations in research and training on rice and paddy production as a basis in the food production system."
The Minister added that the four-year plan document signed now is important to fill and strengthen the shortcomings of the implementation of technical training, research and dissemination of Cambodian rice varieties in order to increase rice production productivity. Improve the quality of rice, strengthen the value chain and enhance competitiveness to achieve the policy of exporting rice to the world market in the near future.
Taking this opportunity, Dr. Yurdi Yasmi thanked the Minister for taking the time to sign the four-year plan document on "Cooperation in Science and Technology Cooperation in Production Research and Training". "Rice and paddy are the basis of the food production system."
Dr. YASMI would like to thank CARDI and the General Department of Agriculture for their excellent cooperation and hope that this cooperation will be strengthened and expanded to contribute to the further development of Cambodia's rice production sector. Expansion of research, dissemination, training, production techniques and selection of varieties and the preparation of important policies related to Cambodian rice production and export.
Please be informed that IRRI has been cooperating with Cambodia on the development of rice production since 1963. After the end of the Pol Pot regime in 1985, cooperation resumed. In 1986, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries signed an MOU with IRRI, which was renewed every five years.
In this cooperation, the Cambodian side has received 4 main benefits: 1: Human resource development, 2: Construction of research and laboratory infrastructure, 3: Research and release of good rice varieties Farmers and fourth: the conservation of Cambodia's traditional rice varieties, more than 6,000 samples to assess and determine the agronomic characteristics, as well as the scientific documentation for the next generation of scientists, etc ..
