May 20, 1973 was the birthday of the cooperatives of the Khmer Rouge Democratic Kampuchea regime and set out eight policies for their leadership. Their eight-point policy includes:
1. Evacuation of the city from Hei is divided into two categories: people who lived in the liberated areas before April 17 are called old people. As for the people who were evacuated from Phnom Penh after April 17, they are called the new people or the April 17 people.
2: Complete elimination of the free market economy
3: Get rid of currency and spending money
4: Eliminate religion, all monks must be decapitated and sent to the fields
5: Killed all the soldiers and officers who worked for the Lon Nol regime
6: Establish cooperatives across the country
7: Expel all Vietnamese from Cambodia
8. Deploy troops along the border, especially the Vietnamese border.
Starting from this policy full of anger and murder, and after conquering Phnom Penh to take full control of the country, the leaders of the Khmer Rouge regime, known as the "Democratic Kampuchea regime", implemented their policies. Extremely wild.
The Khmer Rouge massacred innocent people, forced them to work hard, and suppressed all kinds of freedoms. In just 3 years, 8 months and 20 days (April 17, 1975-January 6, 1979), the Khmer Rouge regime killed nearly 3 million Cambodians for allegedly betraying an organization by starvation. The disease has been eradicated and millions of Cambodians have been tragically lost.
