(Phnom Penh): May 20, 1973 was the birthday of the cooperatives of the Khmer Rouge Democratic Kampuchea regime and continued the war until it reached the control of Phnom Penh on April 17, 1975.
Prior to the Victory Day, April 17, 1975, between May 20 and 25, 1974, the leaders of the Democratic Kampuchea regime, Pol Pot and Nuon Chea, announced their policies. Eight points for their regime.
Their eight-point policy includes:
1: Evacuation of the city from Hei is divided into two categories: people who lived in the liberated areas before April 17 are called old people. As for the people who were evacuated from Phnom Penh after April 17, they are called the new people or the April 17 people.
Starting from this policy full of anger and murder, and only after the conquest of Phnom Penh to take full control of the country, the leaders of the Khmer Rouge regime, nicknamed "Democratic Kampuchea", the leaders of The regime has implemented their policies.
Their brutal policies massacred innocent people, forced them to work hard, and suppressed all kinds of freedoms. In just 3 years, 8 months and 20 days (April 17, 1975-January 6, 1979), the Khmer Rouge regime killed nearly 3 million Cambodians for allegedly betraying an organization by starvation. The disease has been eradicated and millions of Cambodians have been tragically lost.
After the genocidal regime of Pol Pot was overthrown under the Khmer resistance movement led by the CPP on January 7, 1979, on May 20, 1983, the National Assembly of the People's Republic of Cambodia set a date. This is the "Day of Anger" celebration. This day is celebrated in anger at the Khmer Rouge regime, which brutally massacred and tortured the Cambodian people.
After the National Assembly of the People's Republic of Cambodia has determined this, every May 20, the Government of the People's Republic of Cambodia celebrates this Day of Anger, highlighting and commemorating the atrocities and sufferings of the genocidal regime. But after Cambodia established a second kingdom after the 1993 election, "Anger Day" was changed from "angry" to "remembered."
On February 14, 2018, the Royal Government designated May 20 as the "National Day of Remembrance". This day is designated as an official national holiday to commemorate the victims and victims of the crimes committed during the Democratic Kampuchea regime from April 17, 1975 to January 6, 1979.
Article 2 of this sub-decree states that this National Day of Remembrance is designated as a public holiday. Every citizen is given the opportunity to organize ceremonies according to their religious beliefs or customs. The Ministry of Water Resources announces the weather conditions from today to May 26 and warns people to be careful of thunder, lightning and gusts of wind.
