May 20 Remembrance Day was called the "Day of Anger" by the National Assembly of the People's Republic of Kampuchea in 1983 in remembrance of the Khmer Rouge regime's massacre of the Cambodian people. May 20, 1973 was the birthday of the cooperatives of the Khmer Rouge Democratic Kampuchea regime, and the war continued until it reached the control of Phnom Penh on April 17, 1975.
"National Day of Remembrance" has just been designated by the Royal Government of Cambodia as a national holiday on February 14, 2018. This day is designated as an official national holiday to commemorate the victims and victims of the crimes committed during the Democratic Kampuchea regime from April 17, 1975 to January 6, 1979.
