Minister Veng Sakhon led a delegation from the Ministry of Agriculture to pay tribute to the late Senior Minister Him Chhem
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, on the afternoon of May 18, 2020, led a delegation of the Ministry of Agriculture to pay tribute to the late Senior Minister Him Chhem in charge of special missions and former Minister of Cult and Religion. Died on Saturday, May 10, 2020, at the age of 82, due to illness.
Mr. Veng Sakhon and the delegation of the Ministry of Agriculture laid a wreath of incense to pay homage to the late Senior Minister Him Chhem and met with Mrs. Sous Nara Him Chhem and her family in a sad and mournful atmosphere. The death of the Senior Minister.
Afterwards, Mr. Veng Sakhon handed over to Ms. Sous Nara and her family the opportunity to attend the funeral of Senior Minister Him Chhem and continued to sign the Golden Book, which he wrote as the last act of the ceremony. The body of Senior Minister Him Chhem.
Mr. Veng Sakhon considered the death of Senior Minister Him Chhem not only the loss of her husband, her father and grandfather, the family's deepest loved ones, but also the loss of one of the nation's most prominent figures who had given up her power. Body and mind, mind, not afraid to work hard to build a society by promoting the values of culture and Buddhism.
Delegates of the Ministry of Agriculture expressed their deepest condolences, sadness and shock at the death of the Senior Minister and shared this sad death with Mrs. Sous Nara Him Chhem and her family and prayed for the soul. Senior Minister Him Chhem was happy to die, not to be wasted.
Spokesperson of the General Commission: In the past 18 days, 27,702 vehicles were inspected and fined according to the new sub-decree.
(Phnom Penh): During the 18 days of tightening the road traffic law by increasing the fines according to Sub-Decree No. 39, on May 1-18, 2020, the traffic police nationwide inspected and fined 27,702 vehicles in That 20388 motorcycles and 7315 cars. This was confirmed by Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun, Deputy Commissioner General and spokesman for the National Police General Commission, to Fresh News.
According to the lieutenant general, on May 18, 2020, the traffic police inspected and fined 1,034 vehicles, including 775 motorcycles (most of the offenses were without helmets, drunken cell phones) and cars. 256 units, most of the offenses are speeding, no belt and drunk.
Regarding traffic accidents in Cambodia, on May 18, 2020, there were 9 traffic accidents, in which 5 people were killed and 11 people were injured, of which 7 were seriously injured. This is according to the spokesperson of the General Commission of the National Police.
