(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, on Friday morning, May 15, 2020, chaired a meeting of the Council of Ministers to review and discuss a number of agendas.
According to the announcement of Mr. Phay Siphan, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Chairman of the Government Spokesperson Unit, the agenda of this morning's meeting is to discuss and pass on the following agenda:
1: Draft law on anti-financing for the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction.
2: Draft Law on Anti-Money Laundering and Terrorist Financing
3: Draft law on mutual assistance in the field of criminal law.
4: Other issues
* Request for approval from the Council of Ministers to seek approval from the legislature on:
4.1: Draft Law on the Approval of the Agreement on the Elimination of Duplication of Taxes Related to Income Taxes and the Prevention of Tax Evasion and Tax Avoidance between the Kingdom of Cambodia and Malaysia.
4.2: Draft Law on the Approval of the Agreement on the Elimination of Double Taxation Relating to Income Taxes and the Prevention of Tax Evasion and Tax Avoidance between the Kingdom of Cambodia and the Republic of Korea.
4.3: The draft law approving the Kingdom of Cambodia to be a party to the Bern Convention on the Protection of Literary and Artistic Works of 1886 and its 1979 Amendment.
Thailand's Phuket airport to resume normal operation
BANGKOK: The Thai Civil Aviation Authority (CAAT) said in a statement on Thursday that it had given permission for Phuket International Airport to resume operations, with a deadline set for Saturday. According to Xinhua published on the night of May 14.
CAAT said Phuket Airport will allow both domestic and international flights.
However, travelers are required to fill out a form stating their reasons for traveling to Phuket.
Despite the reopening of the airport, international flights are unlikely to land in Phuket, as CAAT has banned all flights landing in Thailand except for emergency measures.
