This is according to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, who met a man named Kruy Chamroeun, who admitted that he actually shot and killed a "Keng Kang Thom" animal during his visit. At Anlong Waterfall Resort, a nine-story waterfall in Monivong Bokor National Park in Kampot province on May 10, 2020.
A man named Kruy Chamroeun wrote in a letter of apology to the public: "Please acknowledge the fact that a" Keng Kang Thom "animal was shot and killed in a nine-story waterfall resort in Techo Aphivath village, Chhouk district, Kampot province. I apologize to all Cambodians, please forgive me and stop making such mistakes. People, please do not follow my example. "I promise to hand over the weapon that shot the lizard to the Ministry of Environment."
Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment Neth Pheaktra stressed that the man had admitted his guilt and asked for forgiveness from the public that he had committed the offense. He said that by acknowledging this mistake, the Ministry of Environment accepted and made a contract to stop this behavior forever. He said this was a bad example for others to avoid.
At the same time, the Secretary of State called on the people who visit natural resorts not to commit the crime of hunting and not eating wild meat and to participate in the protection of wildlife to serve nature tourism and protect biodiversity. Diversity and ecosystems in Cambodia.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra pointed out that Keng Keng is an endangered species and is now found in only a small number of areas, such as Bokor Mountain, Cardamom Mountains, eastern Cambodia.
The "Keng Kang" is a species of bird that is still present and endangered in Cambodia until now, but its numbers have not been studied in detail.
According to the Wildlife Conservation Society, this species of bird is known to have been endemic to Cambodia since 1964 and to this day, although no in-depth study has been conducted. They are like vultures, white-tailed deer and cranes, but their presence in Cambodia is still known and seen.
According to the document, this species of bird is presumed to be present in the mountains in the southwestern part of the country and found some presence in the forest in the northeastern region, especially in Mondulkiri province.
Although this bird is still present in Cambodia, it is a bird with little information. Its presence is recorded in biodiversity studies, often without documentation on the biology and their numbers in the same country.
During a survey of the core protected forest, its presence was also spotted, especially in areas bordering Vietnam, where some of its nests were found.
"Keng Kang" is a bird that likes to live and nest in the forest with big trees and is a bird that eats large fruits. Apart from Cambodia, it is a bird that lives in the forests of the tropics in Southeast Asia, but it is facing decline in Laos and Thailand. So far, it has been a relatively endangered bird due to habitat loss due to deforestation and poaching.
