Microfinance Association of Cambodia: Kovid Crisis 19 Nearly 120,000 customers received credit restructuring with over $ 488 million in loans
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian Microfinance Association (CMA) on May 13, 2020 issued a new press release stating that as of May 10, 2020, 137 clients of microfinance institutions in Cambodia 158 have applied for credit restructuring due to the Kovid 19 crisis.
The Cambodian Microfinance Association of Cambodia, out of all the clients who applied for credit restructuring, 118,182 clients, or nearly 90%, received credit restructuring with a total loan size of over $ 488 million.
According to the Cambodian Microfinance Association, MFIs can only refinance their clients if requested by the clients in accordance with the contract between the institution and the client, the client protection policy, as well as some other legal conditions. In the difficult circumstances caused by Kovid 19, the association has been actively working with its members and stakeholders, especially the National Bank of Cambodia, to assist the affected clients.
The Cambodian Microfinance Association continues to encourage and welcome clients who are genuinely affected by Kovid 19 disease, please contact the institution where they received the loan or through a credit officer directly to obtain an appropriate solution.
Based on the current situation, the association is optimistic that the general condition of Kovid 19 will improve in the near future. The association, along with its members, has been working to prevent the second wave of Kovid 19 from occurring, and we hope that the public will be more vigilant in preventing the effects of the disease.
As of the end of the first quarter of 2020, the microfinance institutions that are members of the association continue to adhere to their vision by helping more than 2.2 million Cambodians and have a total credit of more than 30 trillion riels. $ 7.4 billion) with a total of more than 2.8 million depositors and a total of more than 14 trillion riels ($ 3.6 billion) in access to a wide range of official financial services. Time through a sustainable financial system.
