Commerce Minister Asks Australia to Help Establish E-Commerce Regulations
(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Commerce, Mr. Pan Sosak, and Mr. Pablo Kaing, Australian Ambassador to Cambodia, met on May 12, 2020 to discuss a number of mechanisms to expand trade cooperation between Cambodia and Australia.
During the meeting, the Australian Ambassador highlighted some of the activities that the Australian Government has assisted the Cambodian Ministry of Commerce, such as promoting the development of e-commerce and technical assistance in drafting competition laws. Australia is also studying and planning to graduate in 2021 on a new project focusing on economic development, investment facilitation and trade diversification.
Mr. Pan Sorsak thanked Australia for its cooperation and assistance in supporting development in Cambodia.
The Minister noted that Cambodia and Australia are parties to a number of regional and global free trade agreements, such as the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP) and the ASEAN-Australia and New Zealand (AANZ) Agreement.
The Minister of Commerce reminded that Cambodia and Australia will work together to implement those agreements effectively.
Minister Pan Sosak also requested that Australia consider further assistance in activities including regulatory documents and the development of various components in the e-commerce sector, legal regulations of the competition law, as well as the implementation of the recommendations of the integration strategy. Cambodia Business 2019-2023.
During the meeting, the Minister and the Ambassador also mentioned that Cambodia and Australia should examine the possibility of further expanding bilateral trade. The Australian Ambassador said that an Australian company is also studying the possibility of investing in the rice production chain in Cambodia to supply the Australian market. Nearly 200 Families in Kandal Province Receive Donations from Paragon International School
