(Stung Treng): The endangered white-tailed deer is being threatened by wildlife conservationists who are concerned about the survival of these species in Cambodia. Today, some trout are claimed to be present on Koh Sralay land in Siem Bok district in western Stung Treng province.
Mr. Chao Leng Thol said that while the number of trout seems to be declining in other parts of Cambodia, there is hope for Koh Sralay commune, which his organization recently identified. The number of this species is 38. However, according to Nop Borath, the director of Sma Koh village school and the people living in Koh Sralay commune, it is estimated that there are about 50 white-tailed deer.
Koh Sralay is a commune of Siem Bok district and has a forest geography that has been preserved by the community in the area above, so hundreds of species of animals, including two species of trout, the white-tailed trout and the giant trout, are also present there. Also. The trout is considered to be one of the rarest and most endangered species in Cambodia.
It breeds only once a year, from February to May, with one to three eggs per nest. It prefers to nest in large, tall trees with few deciduous trees. Conservationists are concerned about the extinction of this rare animal, especially since it is only found in the Northeast.
Mr. Chao Leng Thol appealed to all people not to hunt, sell or cook those animals. He added that to protect these endangered birds to survive for future generations to know and study.
