General Neth Savoeun orders all traffic police not to fine vehicles for transporting people's agricultural products (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): General Commissioner of National Police General Neth Savoeun this morning, May 11, 2020 Instructed the Capital-Provincial Commissioner and the Director of the Traffic Police Department to continue to instruct all traffic police officers not to impose fines on processed vehicles for transporting people's agricultural products.
This is according to General Him Yan, Deputy Commissioner General of the National Police, speaking to the media after the meeting to disseminate measures to strengthen the implementation of the law on road traffic.
General Him Yan claimed that in a recent meeting of the Road Traffic Safety Committee, the National Police General Commission requested the Ministry of Public Works and Transport to review the installation of license plates on recycled vehicles, as vehicles are not yet recycled. There is no correct license plate.
Answering a question from a Fresh News reporter, during this time, should traffic police officers fine the owners of those vehicles or just educate them? General Him Yan made it clear that traffic police officers do not fine all vehicles transporting agricultural products.
General HIM Yan stated, "His Excellency the General Commissioner has confirmed that we have not yet imposed restrictions on them. We will allow the Ministry of Public Works to continue their studies and review the license plates for them . Therefore, we favor the type of vehicles used to transport people's agricultural products.
It should be informed that since May 1, 2020, the General Commission of the National Police has launched a campaign to tighten the law on road traffic by increasing the fines under Sub-Decree No. 39 of the Royal Government to reduce traffic accidents in In Cambodia in 2020.
In the past week, the road traffic police have inspected 13,968 offenses and fined vehicles for violating the law. There were 10,815 cases of motorcycles and tricycles, equivalent to 77%, and 3,153 cases of all types of vehicles, equivalent to 23%.
