Spokesperson of the General Commission: National Road Safety Commission Sets Direction to Strictly Implement Sub-Decree No. 39 (Video Inside) (Phnom Penh): National Road Safety Commission Sets Direction to Continuously Implement Sub-Decree No. 39 Regular inspections continue to ensure sustainability to improve shortcomings, making road traffic law enforcement more effective.
This direction was set at a meeting to review and evaluate the restriction of road traffic law in accordance with the spirit of Sub-Decree No. 39 of the Royal Government for 7 days, chaired by Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Interior and Chairman of the National Road Safety Commission. Road on May 7, 2020 at the Ministry of Interior. This is according to the spokesman of the General Commission of the National Police, Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun.
Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun told Fresh News: “The meeting set the direction to continue to strictly implement Sub-Decree 39 by regularly monitoring and ensuring sustainability in order to improve the shortcomings. Tighten road traffic laws more effectively. At the same time, strive to continue to disseminate education on traffic laws and sub-decree No. 39 in all forms.
Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun added that the meeting reviewed the results of the report of the Second Sub-Committee, which stated that for 7 days, the authorities fined a total of 13,968 illegal vehicles, equivalent to 13,968 vehicles. Among them, 18015 motorcycles, tricycles, 3153 vehicles of all kinds.
The most notable offenses are involving motorcycles, not wearing a helmet, not having a license plate, violating traffic signs, overcrowding and using a mobile phone while driving. Notably, the car is not wearing a seat belt, speeding, traffic signals, using a mobile phone while driving and in case of no driver's license.
The meeting also concluded and assessed that the assignment to enforce the traffic law under the content of Sub-Decree No. 39 has affected the traffic situation. In fact, according to the report, the number of accidents, injuries and vehicle damage has decreased significantly.
The spokesperson added: "We also see some negative points in the implementation process due to the interference from the enemy, the pollution, the situation of posting fake information, confusing the road users and the public. "On the other hand, we have seen some delays in responding to these biases, which has given them the opportunity to share information at will, affecting the social environment."
There is also resentment from the people who use the road and the subject of the rebels as an excuse to attack. That is due to the mistakes of a small number of police officers who apply the law beyond the guidelines and are not very active in the actual situation and are also involved in behavior.
At the same time, the authorities also encouraged some social media to explain and in the sense of calling for respect for road traffic laws. In particular, he cooperated with the Ministry of Posts and Telecommunications to strengthen the 117 hotline network to be ready to answer and explain to people who are suspicious when the traffic police fines or related to traffic rules and regulations on the road.
"Our system has been up and running for a while and we have seen a lot of calls asking about fines, driving licenses, overcrowding and windshields," the spokesman said. The National Police Commissioner General, which has a second sub-committee, has organized a quick reaction team from the General Commissioner to the Capital and Provincial Police Commission to clarify the people and immediately respond on social media to various polluting information.
The General Commissioner of the National Police also regularly instructed the Capital and Provincial Police Commissioners to revise the inspection measures by encouraging the implementation of the set principles as well as rehabilitating the system to set the target for paying fines, including behavioral education. Execution of law enforcement officers.
At this point, Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun emphasized, "We also punished officials who made mistakes and educated some officials. In fact, a deputy district inspector in Svay Rieng province was fined, and some officials who performed bad behavior or exceeded the rules were educated.
