In a press conference on "Progress and Continuation of Kampot Provincial Administration" organized by the Spokesperson of the Royal Government, Mr. Phuli claimed that it is the pride of Kampot province, which produces salt and distributes it to people all over Cambodia. Which is a good quality salt.
"Kampot is a salt-producing province where people in Cambodia receive this product from Kampot, which we distribute throughout the country," he said. "In the past, when the salt community came into being, the salt community was also worried that when there was a lot of production, it would be difficult for us to find a market, but in the past, due to climate change and the capital crisis, it was a bit difficult for the salt producers."
According to the Deputy Governor of Kampot Province, the current leadership of the Ministry of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation, together with the Board of Governors of Kampot Province, is working hard to restrict the salt containing chemicals imported from neighboring countries to be distributed in Cambodia. At this point, the management has been urging officials to perform this task well.
In fact, on May 08, 2020, Mr. Chiao Tai, Governor of Kampot Province, organized a meeting with the Director of the Department of Industry regarding the production and distribution of salt. This is according to Mr. Pholy, Deputy Governor of Kampot Province, speaking at a press conference of the Royal Government Spokesperson's Unit.
Mr. Tev Samnang, Deputy Director of the Department of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation, said that in the first four months of 2020, the production of salt in Kampot province was 27,775 tons, of which 12,332 tons were supplied to the market.
Please be informed that according to the official of the Ministry of Industry, Cambodia needs 100,000 tons of salt per year, of which there are three types of salt, including: Salt for consumption in people's homes. , Salt for industry (for product production) and salt for agriculture.
Cambodia has more than 5,000 hectares of salt production area, which is divided into three stages to produce salt. These include: Type 1 for storing water to make the water clear, second: Type of fly or fine rice for increasing the concentration of salt water, and third: Rice For planting.
Currently, Cambodia is divided into two provinces producing salt. The first province: Kampot province has 6 constituencies with about 174 solidarity groups and the second province: Kep province has 5 constituencies and 2 solidarity groups.
