(Phnom Penh): The World Tourism Organization (UNWTO) has been seeking solutions and is gathering input from experts, scholars and member countries to put forward joint measures or solutions to address the impact on tourism. During the Kovid 19 crisis and after the crisis was over.
Experts and the World Tourism Organization (WTO) have identified a number of solutions that Member States may consider to address the impact on tourism during the 19-crisis and post-crisis stages, including: implementation of safety, hygiene and health measures in accordance with the guidelines. By the World Health Organization, through the use of modern technology, post-crisis planning and the use of digital technology in tourism is increasing.
The World Tourism Organization will prepare a detailed joint report highlighting these solutions as an entry point for all member countries.
After the meeting, Mr. Try Chhiv, Director of the Department of International and ASEAN Cooperation, told reporters that so far the World Tourism Organization has not yet found a solution to the impact of tourism on the Kovid crisis. But the tourism practices ahead are in line with the World Health Organization.
After the meeting, the World Tourism Organization (WHO) will gather input from scholars, experts and member countries to analyze and find the safest and most effective joint solution to implement the tourism process, he said. Worldwide.
He added that in the meeting, many inputs, both theoretical and practical, as well as new smart devices for facilitating tourists and tour operators, digital telecommunications work and many other good points.
Finding a solution not only to be used during the Kovid outbreak, the World Tourism Organization will also develop measures to be taken when the Kovid crisis is eradicated from the earth or can be applied for the crisis in the future. The latter, too.
Mr. Try Chhiv stated that for Cambodia, although waiting to follow the resolution of the World Health Organization, but Cambodia has been implementing measures in the country, while there are no new cases and those who In the past, almost all of them have been cured, leaving only two.
