Opinion: The role of pagodas in building Cambodian society
(Phnom Penh): From the beginning until now, Buddhist pagodas have contributed to the development of Cambodian society in almost all areas, including education, health, economy, social affairs, culture and so on. Many leaders, including Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, have a history of being a pagoda boy and receiving training from a pagoda with monks as teachers.
Almost 90% of Cambodians are Buddhists, and pagodas also play an important role in the development of Cambodian society, including:
First, in addition to being a place for religious ceremonies, the pagoda is considered a place to preserve Khmer identity and culture.
Second, the pagoda is a shelter for poor students from the province who do not have enough money to rent a house to study in the city. The pagoda is a shelter for orphans, the elderly, students and the children of the poor.
Third, the pagoda can also act as a hospital for the treatment of mental illness and a meeting place for people of all walks of life, regardless of color, nationality, social status, regardless of poverty or other beliefs. Help build society such as bridges, roads, schools, hospitals, ponds, wells and plant valuable trees, fruit trees for medicine and so on.
Fourth, the pagoda is a storehouse of knowledge, a library, a museum of culture and civilization, regardless of political affiliation. In all eras, pagodas provide literacy, numeracy, and other education necessary for life. For example, our outstanding Cambodian writers and famous artists from ancient times are all trained in pagodas.
Fifth, the pagoda is a place to teach physical education, speech, mind and train the general public to be compassionate, free from greed, guilt, delusion in order to be free from suffering. His and the monks' teachings focused on the social role of parents and children, between teachers and students, between husband and wife, between friends and friends, between employers and employees, between leaders and people, between leaders and lay people.
However, the current pagoda should strengthen and expand its capacity to contribute to building Cambodian society. Elder Chheng Phon, former Minister of Culture, said: “Monasteries should not build too few unnecessary stones. "Let's turn to building stone people, people who have a lot of knowledge, conscience and a strong heart like stone to be better." According to the Discipline Facebook page.
The great elder in educating and promoting the arts and culture recommended that all pagodas must have: Theory School (Pali School), Theory Experimental School (Vipassana School), Library (Archive), Literature Teaching, How to Write Poetry and How to Recite Poetry. Properly covered, it will withstand a great deal of adverse conditions.
Elder Chheng Phon also reminded that monks before cooking should consider, "These bricks are not from lazy people, but from diligent people, of course. So I eat and serve those people.
Buddhist pagodas are very important in relieving the burden of the state economy to some extent and helping to solve social problems. Khmer society will get proud results if there are more pagodas following the recommendation of Elder Chheng Phon. By: Varman Photo: Kim Panharath
(Kampong Chhnang): Since the traffic police started enforcing the new road traffic law from May 1, 2020 to May 6, 2020, helmet vendors in Kampong Chhnang are happy with their sales. Theirs is sold out every day more than before, up to 1 times 10 times.
Vendors claim that in the last few days, helmets have sold 10 times more than usual because customers are worried about the fines imposed by the traffic police who are enforcing the law without understanding. .
Many helmet vendors in Phsar Leu in Kampong Chhnang province said that since May 1, they have sold between 40 and 50 helmets a day. This number exceeds the number they sell every day before the traffic law restrictions up to 10 times, because previously they sold only 2 to 3 hats per day.
On the other hand, a helmet costs only 12,000 riel, but if the traffic police park is up to 60,000 riel, he chose a helmet because it can escape the fines of the authorities and can also protect The life of his child if even in the event of an accident by any means.
