(Phnom Penh): The Governor of Kep Province, Mr. Ken Satha, this morning, May 05, 2020, expressed his concern about the ability of officials after receiving authority from the National Administration to accomplish various tasks at the local level. Ongoing skills training.
At a press conference on "Progress and Continuation of Kep Provincial Administration" organized by the Spokesperson of the Royal Government, Mr. Ken Satha emphasized that the lack of human resources must be recognized, which requires measures to open short-term training. Further mediums can be trained on a local basis.
The governor of Kep province said, "We have to accept the new task given to him, both his understanding and practical skills are limited, we do not think his ability is 100% acceptable. "We need to take measures to open short-term and medium-term training courses, so that we can train at the local level."
According to Mr. Ken Satha, until now, the national level and the specialized departments have not been working smoothly on the transfer of power from the national level to the sub-national level, but both national and sub-national issues are working together to Resolve how to provide good services to the people in accordance with the policy of the Royal Government.
It should be informed that decentralization and deconcentration reforms are important reforms in the field of governance of the Royal Government, focusing on the reorganization of the administrative system at the sub-national level, based on the principles of unified administration and democratic development. This reform has improved the provision of public services and local development in response to the real needs of the people and contributed to the effective reduction of poverty.
For the decentralization and co-decentralization reforms to be successful, the participation of the people in this journey is an essential element. Citizen participation is like a compass to guide the way for sub-national authorities to make comprehensive decisions, leading to local development to meet the needs of the people.
In connection with this reform, in 2020, each commune will receive an average budget of approximately $ 122,000 to make it possible for commune councils to be able to participate in addressing the priority needs of each locality. The $ 120,000 budget includes more than $ 70,000 for local development, more than double the amount in 2019.
In addition, Mr. Ken Satha affirmed that the provincial administration has made efforts to achieve proud results, maintain security for national and international dignitaries, national and international tourists and citizens better, ensure the business, increase income, reduce Special migration reduces poverty from 16.49 percent in 2012 to 3.18 percent in 2020.
Along with the success, the provincial governor also pointed out some shortcomings, including: lack of participation of landowners in the development of their plots, lack of investment from the private sector, especially foreign investors, some investment companies that have received the policy. Unity from the Royal Government has not yet come to implement its projects and development has not yet met the needs of local people.
