(Kampong Chhnang): Many ancient hills left from the Longvek and pre-Longvek period in Longvek commune, Kampong Tralach district, Kampong Chhnang province have now become a luxury tourist destination that attracts both national and international visitors. Learn about the history of each ancient site left over from ancient times.
In fact, on the morning of May 5, 2020, Fresh News reporter from Kampong Chhnang province, Mr. Say Sanya, visited an ancient temple called Tuol Prasat Kdei. This ancient temple is located in a remote forest from the villagers, which is now called the "court temple". This temple is located in Wat village, Longvek commune, Kampong Tralach district, Kampong Chhnang province
An old man who was stationed there said that this hill is an ancient temple built since the time of King Ang Chan. Some of the forms and aesthetics that can be maintained up to now are under the care of the Honorary Minister Dr. Men Sam An, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of National Assembly-Senate Relations and Inspection, as well as other local and foreign philanthropists. Built and maintained since 2004.
The location of this court temple is not inhabited by monks, only one grandfather and two grandmothers are still cleaning the place for Buddhist worship.
Mr. Sok Thuok, Director of the Department of Culture and Fine Arts of Kampong Chhnang Province, said this morning, May 5, 2020, that there are 32 ancient temple sites in Longvek commune, Kampong Tralach district, with some ancient temple sites. Remains from the 7th or 8th century BC before the Longvek period and some hills left during the Longvek period.
Most of the ancient mounds, which do not have temples or other structures as evidence, have not yet been researched, only to be excavated to find evidence in the ground. On some of the hills where the construction is seen, the next generation has just been built.
Most of the hills are also found at the root of many of the boundaries on each hill, but we can not confirm whether these foundations are the foundation of the pagoda or the house of a millionaire. From that generation, yet only excavation can be confirmed.
Separately, an ancient hill called Tuol Kad is now called by the locals as Tuol Court, probably from ancient times, for the trial only to be named today, this hill is also named by Ms. Men Sam An and General Hok Lundy, the former National Police Commissioner, built the site on this hill for worshipers as well as for tourists to visit and get to know the history of this ancient hill.
