Senior Minister Sun Chanthol: Tomorrow, the Ministry will issue a warning letter to the builders of National Road No. 2 and No. 22 after inactivity in construction
(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, said that tomorrow, the Ministry of Public Works will issue a warning letter to the builders of National Road No. 2 and No. 22 after the construction company was late and not technical. And no machinery to do the job.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said this this morning, May 4, 2020, on the occasion of leading a delegation of the Ministry to inspect the progress of the construction of National Road No. 11 with the participation of professional officials, representatives of construction companies, consulting companies. Technical and stakeholders as well.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol continued, "Tomorrow, the construction company of National Road No. 2 and No. 22 will receive a written warning letter from you in person. How much time will be left to resolve this issue? Public works and transportation. National Road No. 2 and No. 22 are inactive, there is nothing to do.
The senior minister further stated that tomorrow, the warning letter will be sent to the construction company and a copy to the Korean Embassy to inform about the lack of work on the construction of National Road No. 2 and No. 22.
Please note that the project to upgrade National Road No. 2 and National Road No. 22 has a total length of 72.17 km, of which National Road No. 2 from Takhmao roundabout to O Chambok is 62.56 km long and National Road No. 22 from O Chambok to Ang Ta Som is 9.61 km long and is located in Takeo Province.
Shinzo Abe declared a state of emergency in Tokyo and six other provinces on April 7, and later decided to declare a state of emergency as the whole country became infected. The measure expires on Wednesday, but Abe has decided to extend it until May 31 after consulting with experts warning that Japan still has a long way to go to overcome COVID-19.
Japan's state of emergency is not as restrictive as in Europe. The move allows authorities to force people to stay indoors and ask some businesses to close. But the authorities can not force the people to comply and there is no penalty for the perpetrators.
