The branch manager appealed to the inspectors to guide all vendors in the general market, all sales of goods, food, daily food, please do not steal chemicals, because it leads to damage to our health. Experts also promote health education through stickers at outlets and other media. Explosive bomb kills man in Sambo district, Kratie province
(Kratie): A self-inflicted accident caused the death of a man at the scene caused by an improvised explosive device. This incident happened on the evening of May 3, 2020 in Koh Chbar village, Koh Khnhe commune, Sambo district, Kratie province.
According to the Sambo district police report, the victim was Teng Sokun, alias Chrum, a 23-year-old male farmer living in the commune.
The victim came from the forest, slept on the roof of the house with a revolver, and on the roof was the victim's mother and 4-year-old brother in the kitchen.
Moments later, a gunshot was heard, and the victim's mother ran out of the kitchen and saw the victim lying dead with a gunshot wound to the left chest.
According to the report, after the incident, the Sambo District Police Inspectorate and the Koh Khnhe Police Station went to the scene to gather information about the incident and examine the body.
After examining the body, the police concluded that the victim really died because the weapon was in the left chest, and the police made a note to hand over the body to the family for the traditional ceremony. Drivers of cars in Ratanakkiri rush to check the technical condition of vehicles as road traffic laws are tightened
(Ratanakkiri): Many cars and trucks come to inspect the technical condition of vehicles at the Department of Public Works and Transport of Ratanakkiri Province while enforcing the road traffic law enforcement from the 1st. May 2020
Acting Director of Ratanakkiri Provincial Department of Public Works and Transport, Mr. Khim Romney, said this morning, May 4, 2020, that many cars and trucks are inspected every day at the inspection center. Vehicle specifications or so-called Chheak is located in Banlung City, Ratanakiri Province.
He said that this number occurred from April 29 to May 3, 2020, causing traffic jams waiting for technical inspections, and in addition to people living in Ratanakkiri. Residents from Stung Treng and Mondulkiri provinces also inspected a total of 191 cars and trucks during the five days, of which 164 were in good condition and 27 were inadequate.
The Acting Director of the Ratanakkiri Provincial Department of Public Works and Transport said that while tightening the law on road traffic, which came into force on May 1, 2020, the Provincial Public Works Department has made every effort to facilitate the people. Who respect the law and come to receive services at the vehicle technical inspection center in Ron Kiri province.
It should be noted that according to the content of Sub-Decree No. 39, Article 3 of the new Article 7, point 4 states that the driver of a light vehicle shall be fined 180,000 Riels and the driver of a heavy vehicle shall be fined 375 Riels. 000 Riel for driving offense without vehicle technical inspection certificate or using an expired technical inspection certificate.
