In addition to the objects enshrined in the Constitution, another important objective of foreign policy is to create opportunities for people to develop their communities.
In order to maintain Cambodia's independence and continue to grow, Cambodia needs a society that is sustainable, environmentally friendly and resilient. This is in line with the vision of the Royal Government of Cambodia to improve the development approach by giving priority to the people to achieve the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development, especially the eradication of poverty, common prosperity and no one. Leave no one Behind.
A society with an environment:
Intervention policy:
Intervention policies may include the organization of open dialogue between stakeholders on specific issues, the promotion of inter-faith dialogue, the establishment of capacity building programs and appropriate support systems for vulnerable groups (such as persons with disabilities, women and children), groups. Discriminatory minorities and groups (such as people living with HIV / AIDS).
A resilient society:
The term "resilience" is defined by United Nations Resolution 71/276 as "the ability of a community or society at risk to withstand, absorb, adapt, change and rehabilitate "Effective and timely hazards, including through the preservation and restoration of its basic structure and core functions through risk management."
Intervention measures include building resilient communities and societies by developing three-step response strategies: disaster / risk prevention, response and rehabilitation.
A sustainable society:
Cambodia should begin to guide and promote policy dialogue on "stakeholder-oriented capitalism", referring to a system in which businesses aim to serve the interests of stakeholders, including customers, suppliers, employees, local communities and shareholders.
