Takeo Provincial Police Commissioner clarified after unscrupulous people posted on social media related to the police force training to patrol to crack down on crimes along the border with Vietnam
(Takeo): A spokesman for the Takeo Provincial Police issued a letter to the public after a number of unscrupulous people posted on social media, spreading false information related to the police force training to prepare for special patrols. Drug offenses along the border with Vietnam.
In this regard, the spokesman of Takeo Provincial Police would like to remind that on August 27-28, 2019, the Ministry of National Defense of the Kingdom of Cambodia and the Ministry of National Defense of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam organized a border friendship meeting. Cambodia-Vietnam) to strengthen cooperation in the prevention and suppression of all kinds of cross-border crimes, especially cross-border drug crimes of the three provinces.
The posting of the above content is completely fictional, contrary to the images and content, which is intended to incite social pollution and cause racism, create insecurity along the border, contrary to the actual activities of the National Police. Strive to maintain security, social order and increase harmony along the border for the people of the two countries.
Local company opens 100-hectare natural shrimp farm in Sihanoukville, Agriculture Minister encourages efforts to expand farming
(Preah Sihanouk): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, accompanied by Mr. Eng Chea San, Royal Government of Cambodia Delegate in charge of Fisheries Administration, Mr. Kong Vithanak, Deputy Governor and colleagues this morning, May 01, 2020. This is to inspect the progress of the development of Heng Heng Company's natural shrimp farm, located in O'Chamnar village, O'Oknha Heng commune, Prey Nob district, Sihanoukville province.
For a fee of $ 10,000 per year (minus expenses), natural shrimp cost $ 5 to $ 7 per kilo. This natural shrimp market is very popular, especially in big restaurants in Sihanoukville and in Phnom Penh. In the future, there are plans to expand the area to raise more hatchlings, prawns and crabs for sale to local fishermen, as well as to establish dried shrimp handicrafts.
In particular, farmers who used to farm the disaster now turn to natural shrimp farming and will expand the area by implementing agricultural production under contract with the company.
Not only that, Mr. Veng Sakhon also expressed his support for the willingness to expand the area in the future, which will contribute to increase natural shrimp products for the local market and processing for export, environmental protection and consumer health.
