Deputy Prime Minister Aun Porn Moniroth: In the context of Kovid 19, the Royal Government and the private sector are facing the problem of traveling together in "one boat, mourning together"
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Economy and Finance and Chairman of the Economic and Financial Policy Committee, on April 30, 2020, the Royal Government of Cambodia has always considered the private sector as an important mechanism in Promote economic growth and create jobs for Cambodians.
The Deputy Prime Minister stressed that in the context of the spread of Kovid-19, the Royal Government and the private sector are facing common problems in continuing to promote sustainable socio-economic development of Cambodia, or in other words, the Royal Government and The private sector is traveling in "one boat, mourning together."
This statement was made by Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth during a meeting with the private sector to review and discuss the "current business situation and necessary measures" in the context of Kovid-19. And is continuing to spread regionally and globally.
The spread of Kovid-19 has been a growing threat to business operations in Cambodia's key economic sectors due to slowing regional and global economic growth, particularly declining demand in external markets, closures. Travel of countries in the region and the world and the effectiveness of the implementation of measures to prevent and prevent the transmission of Kovid-19 disease.
Although the situation of Kovid has gradually improved, the Royal Government of Cambodia continues to pay close attention and proactive in managing this epidemic as well as reducing the negative social and economic impact.
In this sense, contributing to the sharing of adversity and solving common challenges ahead and in helping to restore Cambodia's development during these difficult times is "an indispensable factor." In fact, the Royal Government has already put in place three rounds of intervention measures to manage the impact in key areas, taking into account all aspects of:
* First: Help companies, factories, enterprises and businesses affected so that they can continue their business.
Overall, the private sector appreciates the intervention measures that the Royal Government of Cambodia has put in place so far. At the same time, representatives from key business people also raised the assessment of the current business situation in Cambodia and raised the ongoing challenges in each sector, and also proposed measures for the Royal Government to consider. Contribute to the recovery and promotion of economic growth.
At the same time, the private sector has also expressed its strong will and patience to contribute to the Royal Government in resolving relevant challenges.
Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister and Chairman of the Meeting, emphasized that the Royal Government will consider these inputs to prepare a package of subsequent intervention measures to restore and stimulate the Cambodian economy. The private sector and the government must continue to work closely to prepare for new opportunities that may arise during and after the Kovid-19 crisis.
Finally, he thanked the private sector for its efforts to actively participate and cooperate with the Royal Government in the socio-economic development of Cambodia.
Two economists comment on civil society report on banking and microfinance
Nget Chou, an economist, said the three-month non-payment and collateral withdrawal was unprofessional and could lead to the collapse of the financial sector. Recently, civil society organizations have asked banks and financial institutions Postpone the loan repayment for 3 months and return the collateral for the mortgage to the customer.
"As an independent person with no interest or business in this field, I understand that the application is unprofessional and could pose a risk to the financial sector as a whole," Nget Chour said.
Mr. Nget Chou claimed that:
2: What do customers want the collateral back to do? The justice is that we borrow money to use, we have to keep collateral. If the collateral is allowed to be withdrawn, there may be anarchy, which will lead to the collapse of the entire financial sector. Normal.
But why withdraw the collateral? In general, even if the situation is suspended for 3 months for all customers, there is no reason to withdraw the collateral, and if all plans are withdrawn when the situation improves? Will the customer continue to repay? The answer is no, this is where the sector collapses.
At the same time, Mr. Om Seng Bora encouraged people to find other ways to find money to repay creditors.
