Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen thanked Lok Chumteav Krouch Sam An for donating 1 million Riels to the Cambodian Red Cross for May 8, 2020
(Phnom Penh): The National Committee for Road Safety and the General Commission of the National Police announced on May 1, 2020, to tighten the implementation of the Road Traffic Law, with large fines for violators and violators of the law. Road traffic.
2020 is considered by the Royal Government as an emergency year for traffic accidents after seeing the increase in the number of traffic accidents and the high risk to society and the lives of the people. In order to restrict people to strictly abide by the traffic law in force in Cambodia, the Royal Government has also decided to amend the sub-decree on the level of fines on violators or traffic offenders.
The new sub-decree on fines for traffic offenders, enacted on March 17, 2020, sets a fine of up to twice as much as the old sub-decree and more than three proclamations. To 5 times is also available.
Cambodia's road traffic law has been in place since 2015, but the spirit of compliance seems to be limited for motorists. But after announcing the date of strict enforcement of the road traffic law, it was observed that many people woke up to meet the conditions of vehicles and driving in accordance with the law.
The Ministry of Public Works and Transport's two service centers, the Vehicle Technical Inspection Service or simply called "Chheak" and the driver's license exchange or renewal service, have been busy for about a week.
Heang Sotheayut, spokesman for the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, told Fresh News on April 30, 2020 that within a week, the five vehicle technical inspection service centers in Phnom Penh and service providers Both driver's license exchange and renewal at Aeon Mall is very busy.
Mr. Heang Sotheayut said that some car owners have been waiting for the technical inspection since 3 am.
As of April 29, 2020, there were nearly 10,000 cases related to vehicle technical inspection, driver's license change and other services, the spokesman said. There are about 1,200 cases of changing or renewing a driver's license, 3,800 cases, and there are registration and transfer services.
In the new sub-decree on fines, traffic offenders stipulate that drivers who do not have a driver's license or have their driver's license revoked, suspended or revoked shall be fined 1.2 million riels for light vehicles and A fine of 2.4 million riels for heavy vehicles.
Drivers without a vehicle inspection certificate or using an expired inspection certificate will be fined 180,000 riels for light vehicles and 375,000 riels for heavy vehicles. International Committee of the Red Cross Continues to Send Sanitary Ware to Prevent Kovid 19 in Prison
(Phnom Penh): The International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC) on April 30, 2020, continued to send 7,300 sanitary napkins to the General Department of Prisons to prevent the spread of Kovid 19 disease in prisons.
Jinggi Rey Ambekov, deputy director of the prison project coordinator, led the event. The materials sent to the General Department of Prisons are part of the 20 tons of materials that were signed and handed over to the General Department of Prisons of the Ministry of Interior on April 10, 2020.
On April 10, 2020, the ICRC signed a handover of 20 tons of sanitary ware to the General Department of Prisons to prevent the spread of Kovid 19 disease in prisons at Correctional Center 1 (M1).
The ICRC has prepared a $ 230,000 budget for operations to supply the necessary equipment and supplies and provide technical training to prison authorities.
On the day of the transfer of sanitary ware to prevent 19 cases of Kovid in the prison, the ICRC also supported the training to be conducted by the Rapid Reaction Unit of the General Department of Prisons on "Prevention and Control of Infections for "Kovid 19 and rapid response to management in case of Kovid 19."
Since the outbreak of Kovid 19, the ICRC has been active in supporting the General Department of Prisons to take effective measures to prevent the disease in prisons.
However, there are still many points that need to be addressed in the ICRC humanitarian activities, and therefore the ICRC calls on all humanitarian organizations and institutions that wish to contribute to this humanitarian work to participate as much as possible.
It should be noted that in Cambodia, the ICRC has expanded its mission direction in response to the current situation. The charity is focusing on supporting local authorities to prevent the spread or spread of Kovid 19 in prisons and to ensure that inmates remain in contact with their families.
