In an interview with Fresh News, Heng Vanda, president of the Higher Education Association, said that since the Royal Government decided to close schools to prevent the spread of Kovid 19 in March, private educational institutions have lost revenue. Student fees up to 80%.
The president of the Association of Private Educational Institutions, which has 113 member institutions, said that despite the school's efforts to change its online curriculum, many parents have decided not to pay tuition fees and some have stopped their children. .
In addition to the loss of income from tuition fees, private educational institutions also face staff costs and bank debt repayments.
Mr. Heng Vanda claimed that although the school did not open its doors, the school did not cut staff or teachers because the Royal Government announced that it would be difficult to find staff or teachers. Therefore, the school's monthly current expenses have not been reduced, while the revenue has been lost, which is difficult for the school.
Mr. Vanda also said that in addition to the costs for staff and teachers, all private educational institutions are indebted to banks that have borrowed money to invest in their education.
For Mr. Heng Vanda, if the problem caused by Kovid 19 continues like this, private educational institutions can not stand it and may face closure.
Private educational institutions are seen as making a significant contribution to the Royal Government in recent years in developing the education sector in Cambodia. Private educational institutions have also contributed to the intellectual development of the Khmer people in the last few years.
The Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, also considered private educational institutions as one of the Royal Government's excellent educational partners in the development of the education sector. Samdech Techo Prime Minister also always pays high attention to facilitate the operation of this private educational institution.
If the crisis caused by Kovid 19 caused the collapse of private educational institutions as the owners were concerned, it would be a tragedy.
Mr. Heng Vanda said that private schools rely heavily on tuition fees from students, so in order for schools to continue to stand, only the cooperation of parents.
Mr. Vanda urged parents of all students to support the educational institution by continuing to pay tuition fees to help maintain the operation of the school, which has struggled to develop in recent years.
