Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the leader who brought Cambodia as the 10th member of ASEAN, highlighted that since becoming an ASEAN member, Cambodia has worked closely with other members to build an ASEAN community that is people-centered and Put people at the core of a sense of ownership and shared responsibility.
"Indeed, ASEAN has become a core of Cambodia's foreign policy, which can be reflected in linking Cambodia's political, social and economic development to regional integration and international cooperation," he said.
Samdech Techo added that while Cambodia is committed to achieving its vision of becoming a high middle-income country by 2030 and a developed country by 2050, Cambodia considers the ASEAN Community a source of peace, security and prosperity for the Cambodian people.
It should be noted that after a strong and gentle political pursuit, Cambodia was decided by ASEAN to become the 10th member of its summit in Hanoi, Vietnam on April 30, 1999.
Becoming a member of ASEAN on April 30, 1999 was seen as a diplomatic success for Cambodia. Joining ASEAN also expands its power and enhances its role in the region.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen has stated that there are four reasons why Samdech decided to join Cambodia as a member of ASEAN. The four reasons include:
1. Factors that do not interfere in the internal affairs of member countries: Samdech Techo stated that this factor is the biggest factor that attracts Cambodia to join ASEAN, if in ASEAN just enter to interfere in the internal affairs of Cambodia will not participate. But ASEAN has no big brother, big brother, ASEAN has a principle of non-interference in each other's internal affairs.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen once said that bringing Cambodia into ASEAN is not the achievement of Samdech alone, it is the achievement of the whole Cambodian nation, and Samdech also thanked the Cambodian people for supporting the policy. Samdech's foreigner. The Prime Minister also proposed the display of ASEAN flags in various ministries and institutions.
It should be noted that the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) is a regional organization with ten member countries in Southeast Asia, including Thailand, Malaysia, Singapore, the Philippines, Indonesia, Brunei, Vietnam, Myanmar, Laos and Cambodia.
ASEAN is a regional organization that has facilitated intergovernmental cooperation and facilitated integration among all members since its inception on 8 August 1967. The main purpose of this organization is to promote economic growth, social progress and social development for all members, as well as to safeguard regional stability and provide a mechanism for member countries to resolve conflicts peacefully.
ASEAN covers an area of 4.4 million square kilometers, equivalent to 3% of the total land area. All member countries have a total population of over 600 million, or 8% of the world's population. At present, the average gross domestic product of ASEAN has increased by more than 2.8 trillion US dollars. ASEAN has been compared to the world's fifth largest economy after the United States, China, Japan, Germany and the United Kingdom.
