Koy Kuong, a spokesman for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, told reporters on April 29, 2020 that the 17 Cambodians had been arrested by Thai authorities for drinking.
However, 14 of those arrested were allowed to return home (accommodation in Thailand) because they agreed to pay a fine of 40,000 baht (about $ 1,300) while three others are due to go to court for not paying the fine.
Koy Kuong stated: "He went to a meeting to drink or drink together, they arrested 17 people, and under Thai law, when they break the law, they have to pay a fine and pay a fine of 40,000 baht, I know. Of those 17, 14 had already paid, so they were released to their homes. As for the other three, he refused to pay, so they proceeded to court.
According to Koy Kuong, the Cambodian Embassy in Thailand is following the case of three other Cambodians who were sent to court by Thai authorities. He understands that, however, the Thai court will not punish them and may only decide that the three Cambodians pay the fine.
It should be noted that the Royal Government of Thailand has continued to declare the country under a state of emergency for one more month to prevent and curb the spread of Kovid 19. The country has also imposed a number of measures, including those punishable by up to one year in prison or a fine of about 40,000 baht.
