The law on "governing the nation in a state of emergency" was signed into law by Acting Head of State Samdech Vibol Sena Pheakdey Say Chhum.
In April 2020, the Royal Government of Cambodia, led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, began drafting and drafting a law on "governing the country in a state of emergency" at a time when Cambodia was plagued by the Kovid 19 epidemic. The law was drafted at a rapid pace and was passed by the National Assembly, the Senate and the Constitutional Council in just one month.
The Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo, has emphasized that this law is an important legal tool for Cambodia to govern the country in times of emergency, while currently Cambodia does not have a law for governing the country in case of emergency.
The Royal Government of Cambodia has claimed that the Law on the Management of the Nation in Emergency is designed in the spirit of the new Article 22 of the Cambodian Constitution, which states that "When the nation is in danger, the King declares "Public news puts the nation in a state of emergency after the approval of the Prime Minister, the President of the National Assembly and the President of the Senate."
The new law also provides a legal basis to ensure the implementation of the new Article 22 of the Constitution and a legal basis to determine the authority of the Royal Government to issue the necessary measures that are indispensable to respond to the country's emergency situation. Aims to protect security, public order, protect the life and health of the people, as well as protect property and the environment.
The Law on the Management of Emergencies also sets out the procedures and conditions for declaring a nation in a state of emergency, as well as the necessary measures that the Royal Government has the right to impose during a state of emergency.
The law governing a nation under a state of emergency states that a state of emergency can only be placed when a nation is in danger, such as a war or a foreign invasion. Public health is caused by the spread of disease, serious disruption to national security and public order, as well as serious disasters that threaten or endanger the nation.
The Law on the Management of National Emergencies has five chapters and 12 articles. Article 4, Chapter 3 stipulates that when a nation is declared a state of emergency, the Royal Government has the right to issue the following rules:
11. Prohibit or restrict the distribution or dissemination of information that may cause fear to the public or unrest or that may damage national security or confuse the state of emergency. More appropriate and necessary to respond to emergencies.
This article also states that measures to be taken when a nation is in a state of emergency can be put in place for a particular country or territory. If necessary, the Royal Government may establish a mechanism or delegate authority to any competent authority or use the armed forces to ensure the implementation of the above measures.
In times of war or in other circumstances where national security is in grave danger, a state of emergency may be exercised by martial law.
Article 7 of Chapter 4 on Prosecution states that intentional obstruction or obstruction of emergency response operations is punishable by 1 to 5 years in prison and a fine of 1 million to 5 million riels. . An act of obstruction of operation during a state of emergency is punishable by five to 10 years in prison if the act leads to public unrest or endangers national security.
Article 8 of the same chapter states that intentional acts that do not comply with the measures set forth by the Royal Government, as stated in Article 5, are punishable by imprisonment from one month to one year and a fine of 100,000 to 1 million riels. Separately, the act of non-compliance with measures when the country is in a state of emergency is punishable by 1 to 5 years in prison and a fine of 1 million to 5 million riels if this act leads to public unrest.
Separately, juristic persons who intend to obstruct or obstruct emergency response operations shall be criminally liable as stated in Article 42 (Criminal Liability of Legal Entities) of the Penal Code, with a fine of up to 100 million Riels. 1 billion Riels and one or more additional penalties as stated in Article 168 (additional penalties applicable to legal entities) of the Penal Code.
Legal entities that intentionally do not comply with the measures imposed by the Royal Government as stated in Article 5 shall be criminally liable as stated in Article 42 (Criminal Liability of Legal Entities) of the Penal Code and shall be punished. A fine of 50 million to 500 million riels, as well as one or more additional penalties as stated in Article 168 (additional penalties applicable to legal entities) of the Penal Code.
(Preah Sihanouk): Veal Renh Market is located along National Road 4 in the area of Prey Nob Village, Preah Sihanouk Province. The market was established a long time ago, with many vendors selling.
Veal Renh Market has a galvanized roof and is a place where local people exchange local products frequently every day. Apart from this, passers-by entering and leaving Sihanoukville also often stop to buy various items at this market, such as seafood.
Seeing the reality of the hot weather, especially in the dry season, along with the requests of traders in the market, Mr. Say Sam Al, Minister of Environment and Chairman of the Royal Government Working Group visited Sihanoukville through Mr. Lou Kimchhon. The Director General of Sihanoukville International Port and the Prey Nup District Assistance Team have decided to install a heat pump in Veal Renh Market to help alleviate the hot weather in this market.
Many heat sink fans are now fully equipped. Local authorities and traders in the market also expressed their gratitude for the help.
Vendors say the market is not as hot as it used to be since the fan was installed. They said that now it has partially reduced the heat and made the light brighter.
Mr. Sor Kakada, Prey Nup District Governor, as well as the vendors at Veal Renh Market, expressed their deep gratitude to Mr. Say Say Samal and Mr. Lou Kimchhon, who have always cared about the well-being of the people and traders in the local market in Prey Nup District.
