ASEAN Secretary-General announces measures to address Kovid crisis 19 to revive regional tourism
Lim said the current global epidemic continues to confuse the world and ASEAN. It has claimed many lives and disrupted ASEAN's economy. In ASEAN, about 1,800 deaths and 45,000 infections have been recorded and updated, and so far millions are still at risk of infection.
Across the region, health measures have been significantly expanded, including: shutdowns and vaccinations are being implemented in many countries to prevent the spread of the virus and continue to protect lives and livelihoods.
Despite all of these problems, the health crisis has been having a devastating effect on ASEAN's people and economy, from disrupting trade, supply chain, closing businesses to losing jobs.
In addition, the leaders highlighted the important role of the ASEAN Public Health Emergency Coordination Council (ACCWG-PHE) working group to support the ASEAN Coordinating Council in monitoring the coordination efforts between the pillars in response to And Kovid-19 and present socio-economic challenges.
In fact, within the framework of ASEAN Plus Three, health officials have been working together to facilitate the rapid development and distribution of diagnostic tools, antimicrobials and vaccines in ASEAN.
Once again, the revitalization of tourism has been identified as an urgent priority, requiring the coordination of activities under ASEAN Plus Three cooperation.
This is where ASEAN stands in response to this epidemic. It is indeed an uncertain time, and the challenge for ASEAN now is that ASEAN does not yet know the depth and duration of the crisis, so the magnitude and complexity of the crisis remain uncertain. Worse, the socio-economic impact of the epidemic is getting worse.
He said that in the midst of the global economic crisis, the ASEAN economy is currently projected to grow at least 1% this year. ASEAN tourism will also be affected by this negative economic outlook.
According to the World Tourism Organization, the Asia-Pacific region will be hit hardest by the epidemic, with the number of international tourists in the region falling by 9-12 percent this year. There is no question that other difficulties may arise in the coming months, but the ASEAN region must continue to respond collectively.
ASEAN Tourism Ministers' Joint Statement on Strengthening Cooperation to Strengthen ASEAN Tourism Today underscores ASEAN's readiness and commitment to work towards sector rehabilitation ASEAN tourism as well as the entire ASEAN region.
Lim Chok Hoi encouraged ASEAN officials to begin a proper assessment of the impact of the pandemic on tourism and to develop a long-term strategy for strong and sustainable tourism in the region.
In addition, he encouraged increased information sharing and exchange of good practices to help ASEAN tourism respond better to other epidemics in the future.
In addition, Mr. Lim continued to encourage all people to work together to fight the Kovid-19 pandemic with solidarity, commitment and hope that ASEAN will be successful. .
