The Ministry of Commerce rejects the post that "forced to dump mangoes due to lack of market in the circumstances of Kovit 19"
(Phnom Penh): The Secretariat of the Spokesperson of the Ministry of Commerce on April 25, 2020, came out to reject the news that there was a forced posting of mangoes due to the lack of market in the circumstances of Kovid 19.
According to a statement from the Ministry of Agriculture, after seeing pictures and videos describing the forced disposal of agricultural products as mangoes due to the lack of a market in the circumstances of Kovid 19, a team from the Ministry of Commerce visited. Carefully research this case in order to find out the truth and take specific measures to help the affected farmers.
In fact, after arriving at the location and inspecting the dumped mangoes and questioning the relevant parties, the team found that the location of the incident was in Chamkar Svay, Phsar Treng Troyeung Village, Treng Troyeung Commune, Phnom Sruoch District, Kampong Speu Province. Damaged due to ripening in the package, there are about two tons that must be discarded because the buyer can not accept the purchase due to quality issues.
According to the ministry, they can also be seen in the picture showing the yellow ripe mango, which can not be given the opportunity to be transported to any other place to sell, even to the mango factory, not to mention the export abroad. Delivery time, therefore, requires quality mangoes according to market demand.
The Ministry of Commerce said that currently, despite the circumstances of Kovid 19, Cambodian agricultural products still have markets for both export and domestic markets, as long as the goods are in line with market demand, especially mangoes.
Good quality mangoes The mango confectionery factory collects from 500 riel to 550 riel per kilogram, while the mangoes packaged for export cost up to 900 riel per kilogram. Therefore, as long as there is good quality mango, there is no shortage of market.
Currently in Kampong Speu, there are not many mangoes left in the plantations. Brokers transport mangoes from Kampong Chhnang, Kampong Thom, Kampong Cham, Preah Vihear to sell to large warehouses in Kampong Speu for export and serve the needs of local processing plants. The Ministry of Commerce and relevant institutions are working hard to open more markets for Cambodian agricultural exports to the world market. This is according to the Ministry of Commerce.
The Ministry also urges Facebook fans to consider and gather all the information before posting any pictures or content that negatively affect the efforts of the Royal Government, which is currently implementing policies to promote agriculture and Support and encourage agricultural exports.
