The Ministry of Environment announces legal action against anarchist associations in protected areas (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Environment has announced legal action against NGOs and some unregistered associations or associations. Legal and anarchic activities in protected areas under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Environment.
In a statement issued on April 24, 2020, the Ministry of Environment described the association's illegal activities in the protected areas as "gross violations of the law, taking refuge under the label of environmentalists and natural resources." It is a malicious intent to subvert against the Royal Government on the management and conservation of natural resources only.
In this statement, the Ministry stressed that for any NGO or association that continues to act illegally and illegally in the protected area, the Ministry of Environment has no choice but to take legal action. In particular, the law on protected areas to prevent and stop such activities.
Through this statement, the Ministry also asked the relevant authorities to monitor and take legal action against any NGO or association that acts illegally.
However, the Ministry of Environment also called on NGOs or associations that have been operating illegally in protected areas to stop such activities immediately to avoid legal confrontation.
The Ministry also calls on and encourages national and international non-governmental organizations, associations, development partners and all local communities who love the environment and natural resources to provide closer and stronger cooperation to increase efficiency and preventive activities. Conserve our precious natural resources for generations to come.
In the same statement, the Ministry of Environment emphasized that the Ministry has no interest against those who love and participate in the protection of natural resources, but requires all to respect the law in force and act in accordance with the law and honestly. As a partner to work together to preserve Cambodia's natural resources for generations to come.
The Ministry announces that it welcomes registered NGOs or associations and agreements in accordance with legal procedures to participate in the protection and conservation of natural resources, biodiversity, ecosystems and the improvement of the livelihoods of communities living in and Near protected areas for the cause of all our natural resources.
In this statement, the Ministry of Environment said that the Ministry of Environment is currently managing a system of protected areas, including: protected areas and a total of 60 biodiversity conservation corridors covering an area of more than 7.2 million. Hectares account for about 41% of the total land area of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
1,260 rangers from the Ministry of Environment in each protected area have been patrolling, protecting and conserving natural resources with the participation and good cooperation of local authorities, NGOs, associations and local communities. Night and day.
