(Phnom Penh): The risks and side effects caused by the Covid-19 epidemic are large and extensive. The world is experiencing the largest and unprecedented global economic crisis in history since the 1930s. The forces of world governance and humanity are being tested.
The global economic crisis is escalating due to strict measures adopted by many countries to curb the spread of Covid-19 as global production and supply chains are disrupted and global financial institutions are also facing a crisis. Many low-income households also lack the ability to repay their debts.
Geopolitical tensions in some parts of the world are becoming increasingly complex and dangerous due to increasing power struggles and Covid-19 politicalization, both nationally and internationally. The strategic decline in trust between the major powers is hampering international coordination and joint efforts to address global pandemics and related risks.
The rivalry between the United States and China is deteriorating in the coming months. The Asian Vision Institute (AVI) calls on all countries and international organizations to work together in solidarity to save lives and the lives of the global economy. This is a necessary time for cooperation, not competition. It is a time for collective response, not a time for blame.
Survival has become a key word to describe the current situation in Cambodia. AVI calls on people from all walks of life to be united and patient as we go through this difficult time together.
In addition to socio-economic risks, water shortages are emerging risks that will affect the livelihoods of rural farmers and disrupt the overall food security supply.
All risks are intertwined and systemic, and their impacts are certainly significant. The risks we do not see are social and political problems, most of which are caused by the economic crisis and high unemployment. Decreased political trust and confidence in the government are the most dangerous factors that can lead to social problems and political instability.
We need to recognize that this is a systemic economic crisis that creates intertwined and complex risks and interactions that could lead to the collapse of the entire governance system. We need to be prepared in the long run for the fight against epidemics, as well as the effects and risks. It is Cambodia's long march against epidemics and its long-term struggle for economic recovery.
