In a press conference organized by the Royal Government Spokesperson on the morning of April 22, 2020, Mr. Jean Malin claimed that the law was made in accordance with the Constitution set out in the new Article 22. He said that if this law is not made when the country is in a state of emergency, it will be difficult for the Royal Government to control and determine the rights of the people.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Justice stressed that the law was necessary to protect the public interest, protect the lives of the people, protect national security, stability and public order, as well as protect the national environment. Despite the restrictions on freedoms, this measure is indispensable to protect the public interest.
"In light of the principles of international practice and the provisions of international human rights law, these restrictions are not a violation of human rights," he said. "In the past, there have been allegations that these restrictions violate the rights and freedoms of the people."
According to Mr. Jean Malin, the practice of Cambodia is not different from the practice of other countries in the world, in such a situation requires the introduction of a number of measures that restrict freedom. For the public good, to save the lives of the people.
The spokesman for the Ministry of Justice considered that the concerns of some individuals around the creation of this law is a misunderstanding and did not study the law properly, and the other may be only for political benefit. He added that the allegations were also biased and unfair to Cambodia.
It should be informed that the draft law "Governing the country in a state of emergency" has 5 chapters and 12 articles prepared by the Royal Government to lead the country when it is placed in a state of emergency and reached the Constitutional Council on April 20, 2020 for the institution to review the constitution and the Constitutional Council will have a meeting soon.
The draft law on "governing the country in a state of emergency" passed the Senate by a vote of 54 to 54 on the morning of April 17, 2020 to pave the way for the draft law to reach Announced for use in the near future.
