Jean Malin's response came after some Westerners criticized Cambodia for establishing a "state of emergency" law that would lead to violations of privacy and restrictions on free speech. And criminalize peaceful assembly.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Justice stated: "Why? Because Cambodia's measures are not different from all other countries. Some countries have imposed more severe measures than we have enacted in our laws. "Some countries are closing the whole country, some countries are closing cities or regions, some countries are banning gatherings, house arrests and punishing people who do not comply."
"It can be done and no one accuses it of human rights violations? But when Cambodia has the same need to draft this law to put in place the same measures, and the Cambodian measures are lighter and better, they are criticizing Cambodia for violating human rights. "So this is an unjust critique for Cambodia, a biased critique with two standards and a political critique."
For Jean Malin, the drafting of Cambodia's law on "governing the country in a state of emergency" is in line with international legal standards, which are fundamental to respect for human rights. The law also complies with the Universal Declaration of Human Rights and the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights.
He added that so far, about 70% of countries in the world have taken measures to implement "national emergency management" to fight the 199 Kovid epidemic. In addition, ASEAN countries that have declared a state of emergency include Thailand, Malaysia, the Philippines, while Singapore and Indonesia are implementing measures to ban citizens.
Cambodia's draft law on "governing the country in a state of emergency" will provide a legal basis to ensure the implementation of the new Article 22 of the Constitution and a legal basis to determine the authority of the Royal Government to issue necessary measures that are not Indispensable to respond to the state of the country in an emergency aimed at protecting security, public order, protecting the life and health of the people, as well as protecting property and the environment.
