Ministry of Justice Spokesman Says Rona Smith Criticism Does Not Understand Cambodian Law (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Secretary of State Chin Malin and Justice Ministry Spokesman Rona Smith Criticize Rona Smith Criticism The United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights in Cambodia, on the law on "governing the country in a state of emergency," is a critique of Cambodian law.
He made the remarks during a press conference on "Managing the nation in a state of emergency" organized by the Government Spokesperson's Office at the Council of Ministers on the morning of the 22nd. This April 2020.
Responding to a reporter's question, Jean Malin stated: "Criticism of the UN Special Rapporteur on Human Rights (Rona Smith), I see his point, but all that is said. "This is inconsistent with the content and purpose of our law, meaning that he may not fully understand the true content of our law, as well as the purpose of our legislation."
Mr. Malin emphasized that Ms. Rona Smith's criticism may have been motivated by an over-concern, a vague understanding of the content of Cambodian law, or that she might have misunderstood Cambodian content. He said that Cambodia has a sufficient basis to draft a law on "governing the country in a state of emergency" based on national and international human rights law.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Justice said that the international experience base, which is applied to more than 70 countries around the world, Cambodia is not an isolated case and that the rule of law is in a state of emergency. Cambodia has the necessary arrangements to comply with the provisions of national and international human rights law.
At the same time, he also said that the Royal Government, especially the Ministry of Justice, is happy to meet with Ms. Rona Smith at any time when she visits Cambodia to have a detailed and clear discussion on the true intentions and objectives. The certainty of the law and the content of the law to confirm that the drafting of this law is not a point that violates the rights and freedoms of citizens.
