(Phnom Penh): Kovid 19 has caused a severe crisis around the world, including in Cambodia. This devastating crisis not only affected human health, but also caused the global economy to slow down due to its isolation to stop the spread of Kovid 19.
When people can not go out to earn a living, these families suffer, especially the daily wage they cannot afford to work from home. In Cambodia, tuk-tuk riders and tour guides are facing a crisis.
In Cambodia, there are more than 50,000 people who rely on tuk-tuks to earn a living. According to the Cambodian Tricycle Association, tricycle riders generally earn $ 15 a day.
But due to the epidemic and the lack of demand from commuters, it has allowed tuk-tuk riders to earn as little as $ 2 as Daravuth did.
These crises are not only affecting tricycle riders, but even more than 3,000 tour guides are facing the same fate as tricycle riders.
Rasaven, 37, has been working as a tour guide in Siem Reap since 2016 and has been promoting Cambodia's cultural heritage to Japanese visitors in particular.
However, due to the Kovid-19 crisis, she was forced to stay at home, unable to earn enough money to support her family. "I was devastated when I lost my income," she said.
In support of the communities affected by Kovid-19, Wing Specialized Bank staff and agents, as well as Royal Group staff, have contributed funds to help vulnerable families affected by the outbreak. Of Kovid-19 disease. Through this charity, they have organized a fundraiser for vulnerable families, providing some of the basic necessities to support their livelihoods.
"As a socially responsible institution, we not only consider our own health and safety, but we also take care of you," said Manura Chan, CEO of Wing Specialized Bank. "Around us, they are also affected by the crisis."
"It is important for Wing to contribute to the fight against this epidemic as well as support our community through this difficult time together."
"Your generous support is important not only to our community, but also to motivate others to help protect our community," Manu added.
