In a press conference on the afternoon of April 21, 2020, Senior Minister Osman Hassan said that the month of Ramadan may come on April 24, 2020. Ramadan is a Muslim tradition that requires 14 hours of fasting from sunrise to sunset.
In addition to fasting, Muslims are required to perform a number of other duties, such as meeting at the church at night, worshiping salads on the day of Ramadan, giving alms, and visiting homes. Original.
But fearing that last year's religious obligations would lead to the spread of Kovid 19, the Cambodian Islamic Supreme Council advised Cambodian Muslims not to practice what they used to do. That year. These include vegetarian meals, Tarawad salads (Ramadan night worship) and Salafi worship on the day of Rafaitra, in their respective homes without the need for church gatherings.
Senior Minister Osman Hassan said that in Islam, although it is required to perform the above-mentioned obligations in order to celebrate, it also stipulates that transmission to others will lead to punishment.
For this reason, Osman Hassan insisted that worship by converting to worship in the homes of Muslims would not be a sin.
The senior minister also expressed confidence that all Cambodian Muslims would follow the instructions of the religious leadership to help prevent the spread of Kovid 19.
To help prevent the spread of Kovid 19, a vegetarian dinner between Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Cambodian Muslims, which was held last year, was also canceled this year.
In today's press conference, Mr. Osman Hassan also asked for the understanding of all Khmer Muslims.
According to Osman Hassan, of the 33 Cambodian Muslims who have contracted Kovid 19, only one is still receiving treatment. Khmer Muslims who have not yet been treated in Kampong Chhnang province.
Mr. Osman Hassan also noted that by now, Cambodians of other faiths and Cambodians of Muslim relations have returned to normal and there is no longer any fear of contracting Kovid 19. No.
Mr. Osman Hassan expressed his delight at the return to normalcy of relations between Cambodians, Muslims and other religions in the community.
