The WHO representative in Cambodia recalled some of the deadliest infectious diseases in the history of the world. She claims that the deadly disease was the Spanish flu, which struck between 1918 and 1919 100 years ago and killed between 40 million and 50 million of the estimated 500 million infected people.
According to the representative of the World Health Organization in Cambodia, we have done a good job of responding to 19 cases of Kovid. In fact, so far in Cambodia, there are only 122 cases of this disease, while there are up to 107 people who have been cured and not. There was not a single death. She congratulated Cambodia and considered this good news for the world.
When asked why there are 19 more deaths from Kovid in developed countries than in developing countries, despite the improved health care of developed countries, she replied that because developed countries are in a critical stage. Already. This means that even if a country has a good health care system, if they are not well prepared, the rate of Kovid 19 infection will be even higher.
The number of 13 new infections above is a daily figure of less than 30 cases for eight consecutive days, and of these new cases, seven were imported from abroad, bringing the total number of cases to 1,006. South Korean health authorities also reported two more deaths, bringing the total to 236, up from 2.21 percent.
