The reaction of the Cambodian Permanent Mission to the United Nations and other international organizations in Geneva came after Rhona Smith issued a statement on April 17, 2020 alleging that the draft law On the management of the country is in a state of emergency in Cambodia, endangering human rights.
The Permanent Mission of Cambodia to the United Nations and other international organizations in Geneva emphasized that "Special Rapporteurs who are not UN staff but serve in their own capacity should refrain from making any public statements." "One that can confuse public opinion, which can be judged and which can be considered as an act of interference in the internal affairs of a sovereign state."
2. As the spread of Kovid 19 virus intensifies and accelerates, the leaders of at least 70 countries around the world declare a state of emergency and shut down the country to prevent the spread of the virus. By temporarily restricting freedom of movement, assembly and privacy. Therefore, it is not fair at all to criticize Cambodia for making such a law.
4. The laws of Cambodia adhere to the principles of accountability, proportion, necessity and non-discrimination. The authority given to the government in an emergency is not unlimited. The new Article 86 and Article 102 of the Cambodian Constitution provide for a mechanism for review by two levels of the Cambodian Parliament. In addition, any law enforcement officer who acts arbitrarily and violates this law shall be punished in court.
It should be noted that the "Draft Law on the Management of the Nation in Emergency" has 5 chapters and 12 articles prepared by the Royal Government to lead the country when placed in a state of emergency has been approved by the National Assembly in its full session. This morning, April 10, 2020, and was fully supported by the Senate in the extraordinary session of the Senate on the morning of April 17, 2020.
