Spokesman of the Royal Government: Making laws to govern the country in times of emergency is in accordance with the will of the people stated in the Constitution (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Mr. Phay Siphan, Spokesman of the Royal Government of Cambodia on the morning of April 17 The year 2020 states that the rule of law in times of emergency is in accordance with the will of the Cambodian people as enshrined in the Constitution, not in accordance with the demands or wishes of any NGO.
At a press conference on "Progress and Continuation of the Ministry of Cults and Religions", Mr. Phay Siphan affirmed that the law governing the country in this state of emergency is also to protect the interests of the Cambodian people, not to oppose the people. No one.
Mr. Phay Siphan said, "The Royal Government of Samdech Techo Hun Sen is in accordance with the common will of the people, as stated in the Constitution. In this law, it is not a law that goes against the people, it is not an enemy of the people, it is a response to what the will of the people, not an NGO, not the will of the organization. "Human rights."
"If a country has no law, it is not called a country of the rule of law, so the rule of law is within the rule of law," he said. However, the rules of declaration of emergency are many factors that need to be agreed upon, including the Royal Government, the National Assembly and the Senate just passed the Constitutional Council.
According to Phay Siphan, those who do not want the law on governing the country are in a state of emergency.
It should be noted that 54 out of 54 senators who attended the Extraordinary Session of the Senate on the morning of April 17, 2020 have fully approved the draft law on "governing the country in a state of emergency" to pave the way. Let this draft law reach its promulgation in the near future.
The Extraordinary Session of the Senate to review and comment on the draft law was chaired by Mr. Sim Ka, Acting President of the Senate, with the participation of 54 senators and defended the draft law by Mr. Keut Rith, Minister of Justice. Well.
