According to the letter from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the Permanent Mission of the Kingdom of Cambodia provided the following 11 clarifications:
2. The International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights and the Universal Declaration of Human Rights provide for the temporary restriction of certain rights and freedoms in an emergency. But those restrictions will be set by law for the public good and the common good.
3. Cambodia adheres to policies and practical measures to prevent and curb the spread of this deadly virus. Among other measures that have been put in place so far is the drafting of a law on "governing the country in an emergency" that is currently being considered by the Senate.
4. While Article 22 of the Constitution allows for the declaration of a state of emergency, the law on declaring a state of emergency does not yet exist, and this is the reason for the current draft law.
5. This law is permitted to be used by national and international instruments on national human rights, such as the Constitution of Cambodia (new Article 22, 31, Articles 86 and 102), Universal Declaration of Human Rights (Article 29), ICCPR (Article 4). Interpretation of the UN Human Rights Council, including the decision of the International Court of Justice, etc.
7. When applying the law, the principles of accountability, proportion and necessity must be applied. The power given to the government to take action when a country is in a state of emergency is not unlimited. The new Article 86 and Article 102 of the Constitution provide for mechanisms for review by both houses of the Cambodian Parliament. In addition, law enforcement officers who act arbitrarily and violate the law will be punished by the court.
9. Cambodia is committed to fulfilling its obligations under Article 4 of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights if the country is declared in a state of emergency and the law on "governing the country in a state of emergency" is implemented for the benefit of all. Public interest.
