Samdech Techo Hun Sen, a Cambodian leader who bravely sacrificed to overthrow this brutal regime through Facebook, highlighted that today, 45 years ago, was the day that the black-shirt regime forced the people in The whole of Phnom Penh was evacuated to the provinces and then they evacuated people from the provinces to the countryside and were forced to work like cattle day and night without a day off and reunited with their families.
The Prime Minister emphasized that the rights and freedoms of citizens, including the right to survive in the tyrannical regime, are non-existent. Even wearing clothes, they are required to wear only one black, one color.
Samdech Techo, the founder of peace in Cambodia, reminded that in addition to being forced to work without a day off, this brutal regime tortured and beat people to death at will, accusing them of being enemies. In that era, everything was abandoned, including homes, schools, pagodas, hospitals and banks.
The head of the Royal Government continued that under the rule and leadership of the Pol Pot regime for 3 years, 8 months and 20 days, more than 3 million people lost their lives and at that time no human rights organization was present. Come and help Cambodia.
It should be noted that the genocidal regime of Pol Pot was born on April 17, 1975, after the overthrow of the Republic of Lon Nol.
This brutal and brutal regime was overthrown on January 7, 1979 under the resistance movement of the current Cambodian People's Party, along with many other Cambodian patriots.
(Phnom Penh): April 17, 2020, is the 45th anniversary of the sad and tragic event in the history of Cambodia. It was the day that the genocidal regime of Pol Pot took over the country and pursued a policy of evacuating people from Phnom Penh and torturing all kinds of Cambodians.
This tragic event, although almost half a century has passed, has not faded from the memory of the people who went through this brutal regime. Hel Hiep is a Cambodian citizen who went through this regime and she vividly remembers the events of the evacuation from Phnom Penh and the torture of the Khmer Rouge regime on her family and other Cambodians.
Ms. Hel Heap recalled that on April 17, 1975, she and her family fled their homes in Phnom Penh by force of the Khmer Rouge to her mother's hometown in Prey Veng province. She recalled that she and her family took half a month to travel to their hometown on foot.
She recalled painfully that when she arrived home, she and her husband were subjected to all kinds of abuse by the Khmer Rouge. She recalled that as soon as she arrived home, she was forced to cut her long hair to suit the Khmer Rouge regime, and her husband was forced to bark at dogs.
After living in her mother's hometown for a short time, she fled to her father's hometown, which is almost 10 kilometers away. The escape was to escape the acquaintances as well as her maternal siblings, who threatened her life and that of her family as Phnom Penh residents or feudal lords, as alleged by the Khmer Rouge regime.
Ms. Hel Heap also remembers many other tragic stories during the Pol Pot genocidal regime that happened to her and her family. She recalled that the most shocking thing for her was that her husband was dragged away by a cart instead of a cow, and her nails were nailed to a board, and her husband was bleeding profusely.
To know exactly how Hel Hiep and her family suffered during the Khmer Rouge regime? Watch her description in a short video prepared by DC-CAM:
On April 17, 2020, the people of Khemara Moulmey are happy together as some people return from their hometowns to work in other places, especially in Phnom Penh, the heart of Phnom Penh. People, students have returned from their hometowns to perform their duties regularly. Regeneration.
This scene shows the stark contrast from April 17, 1975, when Cambodians in Phnom Penh were forced to leave their homes in misery and as a starting point for the deprivation of Cambodians.
The scene between April 17, 2020 and April 17, 1975 shows the contrast between the shadow of peace, bringing peace and hardship from the fires of war under two different political lines between current and generational politicians.
Let the Cambodian nation know only peace, happiness, and not to face all the sorrows and misery like in the past.
The prospect of a new regime emerging from the Khmer Rouge revolution that would lead them to peace for the better turned out to be one of the most brutal regimes in the world.
The unrest began on the morning of April 17, 1975, when black-clad Khmer Rouge communist troops were seen storming Phnom Penh from all directions.
The last battle on April 17, the army of the Khmer Republic raised the white flag and surrendered. As for the people in all the cities, they came out to welcome the arrival of the Khmer Rouge army, dressed in black suits, carrying all kinds of weapons, including AK-47 grenades and grenades. The people of Phnom Penh are happy and hope that the nation, which has been in crisis for nearly 10 years, will be restored to peace because the Khmer Rouge army is under a government headed by Sihanouk.
Since coming to power on April 17, 1975, the Khmer Rouge has continued to call itself the "Royal Government of National Unity of Cambodia", but the real power is not in the hands of this United National Government, but in the hands of the Khmer people. Red alone.
What is remarkable is that the Khmer Rouge soldiers in black, mostly children, were between 16 and 17 years old, and they had strong faces and did not smile at all. The soldiers ordered the people to leave the city in two or three days without having to carry much supplies, as they could return home in two or three days.
People who walk slowly are shot dead by Khmer Rouge soldiers on the streets, and hundreds of thousands of people trying to leave the city on orders know that they are unlikely to return to their parents' homes. Some of the elderly fell ill, slept on the streets, and even the sick were forced to leave the hospital with hundreds of thousands of others.
During the 3 years, 8 months and 20 days of the Black Shirt regime ruled by the genocidal Pol Pot, Nuon Chea, Khieu Samphan, Ieng Sary and Ieng Thirith, more than 1.7 million to 2.2 million Cambodians Killed by killings, starvation, disease, and other inhumane acts. In addition to massacres, this genocidal regime has left Cambodia devastated and devastated, both in terms of infrastructure and the national economy.
