Plans to start raising fish in the village began in mid-March under the guise of a man known as the adoptive father, Him Won. "I came up with the idea for a pond," Vun said. He explained that because access to certain foods may be in short supply during an epidemic, "we can raise some animals ourselves."
He pointed out that in addition to improving self-sufficiency in Srah village, other resources could also be supplemented. In particular, there is an organic vegetable garden that is designed to be self-sufficient. "We already have an organic vegetable garden, so it seems like another natural addition," Vun said. In fact, the pond and the organic vegetable garden are next to each other.
This 8-by-12-meter fish pond is not easy to set up, especially in the dry season when the temperature reaches 40 degrees Celsius and the soil is hard to dig. But young people in Sovann Komar are happy to build this pond, with 10 young people helping to measure and test before filling the pond with 2,000 tilapia fry.
Managers at Sovann Komar know the importance of such activities, which can help improve the well-being of more than 50 young people by enabling them to be self-sufficient and take care of their food sources.
Vun added that by raising children in Sovann, children can gain a better understanding of where their food comes from and learn valuable life skills. Although they do not plan to become farmers, there are many lessons they can learn from creating their own food source.
Fish ponds and organic vegetable gardens are just one of the many strengthening activities happening in Komar Komar village. Young people can choose from entertainment classes including dance, music, haircuts, yoga, visual arts, martial arts and even join the newly created book club.
In addition, the newly created "Tuesday Career Program" helps young people plan for the future by listening to the stories of many young professionals, from architecture to pharmacies, talking about their careers.
