Ta Uon Chea Temple, one of the 102 hospitals of Jayavarman VII, is undergoing renovations
(Siem Reap): Prasat Prey Prasat or Prasat Ta Oun is one of the 102 hospitals in the reign of Jayavarman VII, built in the late 12th century. The temple is built in the Bayon style, dedicated to Mahayana Buddhism, and is located about 800 meters northwest of the Angkor Thom wall in Nokor Krao village, Kork Chak commune, Siem Reap city, Siem Reap province.
Currently, the temple structure is badly damaged due to age, weather and people who need to be repaired in time, that is, the temple has never been repaired.
Kean Panhavantey, an architect of the Ta Uon temple restoration project of the Department of Temple Conservation in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology, said the temple was at high risk. The main cause of damage to the temple is man-made. To save the temple from danger, the Apsara Authority opened a site to repair and move the stone at the eastern entrance.
Ms. Panha Vandy continued that the team has completed the work of collecting the temple plan, taking photos and compiling documents. The next task is to remove the rocks on the east side to repair and reinforce, and then excavate to study the foundations, compress the foundations, and then assemble the stones into place.
Finally, the team will study the risk points in other corners to withstand the emergency or push the rocks in some parts back to the original position to ensure safety and reduce the risk of the temple.
