The WHO was established to coordinate international health policies, particularly those related to communicable diseases. The organization has a total of 194 member states. Each state appoints a delegation of leaders and health professionals to represent their countries at the World Health Assembly, which is responsible for policy-making and decision-making for the WHO.
Member States have direct control over the leadership and direction of the WHO, the World Health Assembly appoints the Secretary-General of the WHO, sets the agenda as well as priorities, and evaluates and approves the organization's budget. Among many other tasks.
WHO is headquartered in Africa, North and South America, Southeast Asia, Europe, the eastern Mediterranean and the western Pacific, with headquarters in Geneva, Switzerland. According to the WHO website, the World Health Organization has more than 150 representative offices around the world where its staff work with local authorities to provide support as well as health care guidance.
The WHO has a key role to play in promoting a global health care system, closely monitoring public health risks, coordinating health emergency response measures, and promoting the health and well-being of the world's population.
